# Testing

## Definition of done

- Behavior changes require automated coverage when the project has a test suite.
- Never skip, mute, quarantine, weaken, or rewrite a test merely to make a gate pass.
- Never use `--no-verify`, reduced coverage thresholds, focused-only suites, unreviewed
  snapshots, or CI exclusions as "repairs."
- Never hide pre-existing failures. Record them; hard-stop if they prevent proving the
  contract.
- Mock external boundaries (network, paid APIs, production DBs). NEVER hit live production
  services from tests.

## Phase 2 — test design

- **Bug fix:** write a failing regression test FIRST in the correct layer (unit /
  integration / UI / E2E per project layout). Name it after the bug. If the bug reveals a
  systemic gap, propose the accompanying rule/doc change alongside the test — do not fix
  silently.
- **Feature:** design acceptance criteria or BDD-style scenarios (Given/When/Then) before
  writing implementation tests. Cover happy path, failure, and material edge cases named in
  the implementation contract.

## Phase 4 — verify

- Discover verification commands from the project's existing sources: `README.md`,
  `package.json` scripts, `Makefile`, `justfile`, `pyproject.toml`, or CI workflows.
  Prefer the project's documented fast/local gate for iteration and its full/CI-parity gate
  before finalizing an approved-plan run. Never invent script names from another project.
- Record the exact commands used in the implementation contract and handoff.
- After runtime-affecting review fixes (Phase 4b), re-run affected targeted tests and the
  relevant project gates.

## Gate hygiene

- Treat the project's pre-commit / CI gates as authoritative when present.
- If no automated suite exists, state that explicitly in the handoff and propose the smallest
  useful test addition — do not claim green gates that were never run.
